(Phnom Penh): Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh, on the afternoon of March 12, 2020, told all garbage collection companies that wish to bid for garbage collection in Phnom Penh must find out in advance about the nature of the release. People's garbage before deciding to participate in the auction.
During the conference on the procurement process and procedures for the selection of solid waste collection and transportation service providers in Phnom Penh in the first phase, Mr. Khuong Sreng confirmed that the company also needs to find out where to dispose of garbage. He said that once the company has bid, it will hold an additional discussion on the character of the people to dispose of garbage.
According to Mr. Khuong Sreng, the plan is to continue to enforce the law on the disposal of people's garbage in order to ensure that people dispose of garbage in accordance with the regulations and technical specifications to improve the beauty of the environment in Phnom Penh. .
The Governor of Phnom Penh also confirmed that Phnom Penh will be divided into 3 areas for companies to choose to provide solid waste collection and transportation services to the three different areas, and the procurement process and procedures will be Go through two stages, the first is to evaluate the selection of three companies to provide services in the designated areas.
Mr. Khuong Sreng added that for the bidding process for the garbage collection company, the Phnom Penh Capital Administration will stop accepting applications on March 31, 2020, so all companies interested in participating in the bidding process should hurry to complete the documents. Properly follow the prescribed procedure.
It should be noted that after frequent problems related to garbage collection in Phnom Penh, the Royal Government of Cambodia has revoked the right to collect garbage from Cintri Company and take control, with the Royal Government establishing the Phnom Penh Autonomous Authority for Garbage Management and Construction to manage this work. Eleven Thais cross COVID-19 from Hong Kong tourists partying together
