Interior Ministry Spokesman: A total of three people have been arrested in connection with the posting of false information about Covid-19 (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): General Khieu Sopheak, spokesman for the Ministry of Interior, said that until Currently, Cambodia has arrested three people in connection with the posting of false information about 19 cases of Kovid, causing confusion, one of whom has been educated and released, and two of whom have been charged in court.
"So far, we have detained three of them, one of them was released, one was sent to court and the other was sent to court yesterday," he said. "
Since the world first broke out in 19 cases of Kovid, starting in Wuhan, China, some Facebook users, especially anti-government groups, have fabricated false information that Cambodia has had 19 cases of Kovid. Once in this province, once in that province. The posting made Cambodians even more frightened, and some even went to buy food and stockpiled goods.
After seeing such false information posted, the Royal Government of Cambodia has taken a series of actions, including interpreting the true information and taking legal action against the person who posted the fabricated information about Kovid.
Mr. Khieu Sopheak stated that for Facebook, which is in the country and posts fake information to scare people, it will not be able to escape the law because the Ministry has a technical team and has the cooperation of Facebook to research. Those. However, Cambodia has consequences if you post fake information abroad.
He called on the Cambodian people, both inside and outside the country, to participate in maintaining the highest national interest of Cambodia, because Cambodia has gone through many regimes to achieve peace like today. He warned that if any Cambodian has lived in a free country, please do not underestimate the Cambodian people living in the country, let the people in the country be happy.
"This dissemination of false information is very dangerous to our society," he added.
Ministry is developing a technology system to manage truck weighing stations to increase the efficiency of managing overweight
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT) is preparing a technology management system at truck weighing stations across the country to increase the efficiency of overweight management, which is one of the main problems in road damage in Cambodia today.
The Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, has set up weighing stations on national roads to prevent overweight trucks from causing damage. Currently, there are up to 28 weighing stations nationwide.
On March 11, 2020, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT) held a meeting on the progress of the management and inspection of overweight vehicles. The meeting was chaired by Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, and was attended by members of the Overweight Transport Inspection Steering Committee, the Standing Coordinating Committee and relevant officials under the Ministry.
In this meeting, the Senior Minister reminded all officials at the weighing station to respect and strictly follow the law to prevent overweight transport due to the Royal Government's efforts to raise funds to build roads. .
In relation to this overweight, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol used to exchange truck weighing station officials throughout the country. Despite warnings of resignation, the management of the overweight truck was still hampered and ineffective.
In order to improve the management of truck weighing stations and overweight trucks on the streets, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, one of the leading ministries in the use of technology systems for the provision of various services, is also developing technology systems to manage truck weighing stations. Also.
Heang Sotheayut, a spokesman for the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, told Fresh News that the technology had been studied for more than a year. Weighing station management technology system is designed to respond to the current situation of vehicle weighing station management.
According to Mr. Sothea Yuth, the current situation of the weighing station is that officials conspire with traders who do not weigh cars, vehicles do not enter the weighing station or powerful groups intimidate and threaten station officials.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Public Works and Transport said the technology system was scheduled to be launched in the middle of this year with two stations on the outskirts of Phnom Penh and could reach 10 stations by the end of the year.
Mr. Sothea Yut confirmed that the technology system will monitor vehicles traveling on the road and monitor the performance of station officials. Mr. Heang Sotheayut claimed that the technology system developed by the Ministry will monitor overweight trucks and not weigh them, and also monitor and count all vehicles entering the station.
Mr. Sothea Yut believes that once the technology is in place, it will be more effective to manage overweight trucks. The weight on the car. More specifically, overweight trucks can not escape this system.
