(Phnom Penh): Operation Day 3: Authorities inspected drunk drivers throughout Phnom Penh and found 24 drunk drivers and 11 motorcyclists. This is according to the report of the Phnom Penh Municipal Police, which was received by Fresh News on the morning of March 12, 2020.
According to the report, all of the alcoholics were referred to the land traffic office.
It should be informed that the operation to control driving under the influence of alcohol in the middle of the night on March 12, 2020, led by Lt. Gen. Thet, Deputy Commissioner of the National Police and Phnom Penh Municipal Police Commissioner. Last night's operation took place in Khan 7 Makara, Sen Sok, Daun Penh, Dangkor, Chamkarmon, Chroy Changva and Khan Kambol.
Phnom Penh Municipal Police Commissioner Lt. Gen. Thet has stated that this measure is a contribution to preventing driving under the influence of alcohol, which is a consequence of traffic accidents.
Road accidents are a cause for concern because of the high rate of accidents. In 2019, there were 4,121 traffic accidents, killing 1,981 people and injuring 6,141 people. Of the more than 4,000 accidents, about 5 percent were caused by drunk driving.
According to the new Article 77 of the Road Traffic Law, anyone who drives a vehicle while under the influence of alcohol with an alcohol content of 0.40 mg per liter of air or from 0.80 grams per liter. Blood and above is fined 200,000 Riels for motorcyclists, tricycles or tuk-tuk drivers, 400,000 Riels for light vehicles and 800,000 Riels for heavy vehicles.
Drunk drivers who have already been fined once but continue to commit misdemeanors within six months of the previous fine will be sentenced to one to six months in prison and fined. 800,000 to 4 million riels. 10th grade student of Champouvorn High School, Phong Pichraphanut, insists on seeking justice after her father was imprisoned
Please be informed that a police officer working in the Police Technical and Scientific Department of the Ministry of Interior and his eldest daughter were arrested by the police on suspicion of violence against a girl student at Chumpouvorn School on March 3, 2020.
Phanut, the youngest daughter in a dispute with another group of students, raised ten fingers to plead with the court and Justice Ministry leaders to help release her father, claiming that his father had not beaten him, but had arrested him. And only stop those kids.
The daughter said that her father, in addition to working as a police officer, also took the time to run a tricycle to support the whole family, so if the father was imprisoned, who could support him?
According to the girl, Phanut said that she was attacked by a group of girls and that she could not defend herself, so she called her father and she would be sorry if she did not. Call told her father did not hurt her father either.
She asked the authorities to release her father, who was innocent, to take care of her and her sister to give them warmth as before, because she has only one father, no mother like other children. . The two sisters raised ten fingers and begged the leaders of the Ministry of Justice and the court to help her father return home to give them warmth.
The incident happened on March 3, 2020, when the youngest daughter called her father for help during a school dispute. He immediately arrested and detained the group of children, but was accused by the students of beating other schoolgirls until the local police arrested and sent them to court.
