The delegation gave some suffixes for further achievements, distributed study materials to students, handed out scarves to the grandparents who participated and invited to cut the ribbon to be used in the Buddha image and achievements from now on. Nearly 300 Cyclo Riders Receive Donations from Women Donors on International Women's Day
(Phnom Penh): Sharing culture is a good culture of Cambodia that our people always give to each other in order to contribute to good deeds in society and as an activity to teach the next generation to love others. .
In general, this culture of sharing is expressed in different ways, with the sharer being able to provide knowledge, money, materials and consumables. In fact, on the afternoon of March 8, 2020, 280 cyclo riders who are members of the Cambodian Cyclo Riders Association received a charity gift from a philanthropist named Srey Oun.
Srey Oun said that this is not the first time she has done a humanitarian program by distributing such gifts. She said that the reason for choosing International Women's Day as a day to distribute gifts because today is a global holiday that she wants to create a big event is to share this donation to promote a culture of sharing more widely.
She sees that our Cambodian sharing culture is booming and when all citizens have the same idea of sharing, it makes the needy very happy and happy. Srey Oun, the director of Natural Beauty Company, claims that she always teaches her staff to adhere to the sharing culture, because if you want to be successful, you must do something for others, not just yourself.
"In terms of natural beauty, we have a shared culture," she said. In the workshops that I teach all of them, I also teach about shared culture. I told them that if you all want to succeed, do not do it for yourself, do it for all. This culture is what I instilled in them. ”
According to Srey Oun, the donation includes a hammock, a krama, a noodle and some budget packages, and the reason for the hammock was that when they met the cyclo runners last time, they asked because they slept too much every night and were bitten by mosquitoes. In addition to a large amount of personal money, members of Natural Beauty also donated generously.
She points out that she was born into a poor family and did not get much education, even though she wanted to. As a child, she always wanted to create a charity program by sharing such gifts, and now she says that dream has come true.
(Pursat): The Royal Governments of Cambodia and Thailand plan to establish a number of border crossings into international channels to increase trade cooperation and trade between the two countries. The border crossing between Cambodia and Thailand is located in Thmor Da commune, Veal Veng district, Pursat province, adjacent to Trat province, Thailand.
Nowadays, in addition to allowing border residents to trade goods, seek health services and other communications, Cambodians visiting the Thmor Da resort area also use the port to visit Trat, Thailand. .
Try Pheap Group has partnered with a Chinese company to invest hundreds of millions of dollars in the development of this quiet border area. Currently, the company has already built a luxury hotel and is building another large hotel as well as residential construction. According to the plan, the company will set up a golf course as a natural and agro-tourism venue.
Tycoon Try Pheap, CEO of Try Pheap Group, sees that the establishment of the Thmar Da border checkpoint as an international gateway will contribute to making the Thmar Da border area more bustling, not only as a place to exchange goods but also to attract visitors. Tourists from Thailand to visit the rocky area as well.
Tycoon Try Pheap said that Trat is a coastal province and many tourists visit Trat, while in Thmor Da, a natural tourist area with waterfalls and mountain beauty can attract tourists from the side. Trat province to come and relax and play in the rocky area more.
Tycoon Try Pheap stated, "It would be good to open this border gate to the international gate. I see that the opening of this port will not only serve the transportation between the two countries, but it will attract more tourists to the rocky area as we build hotels and resorts ready to receive you. Those tourists already. ”
Tycoon Try Pheap expressed his belief that the opening of the Thmar Da border crossing to the international border would contribute to making Pursat a province with high economic potential and also to improve the lives of the people in the Thmar Da area.
Mr. Nhem Pei is a resident who sells food to tourists who visit the Thmar Da waterfall not far from the border. Nhem Pei, along with his wife, often rides a motorbike to the Thai market, about 10 km from the border, to buy vegetables, fish and meat for the tourists to visit at Thmar Da Waterfall.
Uon Pang is another resident of Thmor Da commune who frequently travels to and from Thailand for consumer goods and food. Mr. Uon Pang saw that the opening of Thmar Da to the international port will facilitate the entry and exit of people to buy consumer goods on time and on demand without the current hindrance.
Pursat provincial governor Mao Thunin has expressed strong support for the Cambodian and Thai governments' plans to open the Thmar Da border as an international gateway. The provincial governor said that the opening of the Thmar Da border checkpoint as an international gateway will be an important economic lifeblood for the province and its people.
"It is important for our farmers to trade agricultural products between Pursat and Trat or between our country and Thailand in any way we can to improve the lives of the people," he said. "
Mr. Mao Thunin pointed out that the provincial authorities and the people of Pursat province are always looking forward to the opening of Thmar Da to an international port. "As provincial authorities, as well as on behalf of all our people in the province, we are always looking forward to the development of this port and the opening of this port. "Because, as I said, this channel is a breath of fresh air for our people and for all sectors."
Although the Royal Governments of Cambodia and Thailand have agreed to open the Thmar Da border crossing to an international gate, the process of opening it has not yet materialized. Currently, the Cambodian side has cleared some land reserved for various administrative buildings at the entrance, but on the Thai side, it does not seem to have seen any preparation yet.
The opening of the rocky border is seen as facilitating the transportation of Cambodian goods made in Pursat, Battambang or Banteay Meanchey provinces to the port, because in Trat there is a deep water port and not far away. Not much from the rocky border.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, said that National Road 55 has a total length of 182.16 km, connecting Pursat through Veal Veng district to the Cambodian-Thai border. This road was built in accordance with the level 3 road standard of the technical standard of the People's Republic of China with a total width of 9 meters, double layer of paved road (DBST) 8 meters wide.
The minister also said that the road will be built at a total cost of 132.80 million US dollars, financed by the Chinese government, together with the contribution of the Royal Government of Cambodia for dealing with the impact of demining, unexploded ordnance, taxes. And tribute. National Road No. 55 took 38 months to build from March 2015 to May 2018.
National Road No. 55 can be considered as the first road in Cambodia, which was built to bend through Kiributta and other high forests along the Cardamom Mountains, making it a beautiful and admirable scenic spot.
Mr. Sun Chanthol said that National Road No. 10 cost a total of 188.38 million US dollars, financed by the Chinese government, together with the Royal Government of Cambodia's contribution to deal with the impact of demining, unexploded ordnance, other taxes. And tribute.
"There is a road of hope" is the motto and vision of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which is set out towards the construction of road and bridge infrastructure to achieve the plan to connect Cambodia's administrative areas, transportation and people's travel. "There is a path of hope" is also a slogan that shows that there is a path that will lead to hope for the growth of both the national economy and the economy of the people.
Veal Veng district and the border area of Thmor Da in Pursat province, which was also the site of a former Khmer Rouge war zone, could be considered a remote area because there were no roads to travel. If people in this area want to travel to Pursat, it takes many nights and many days.
Because there is no road, even though these areas are beautiful, surrounded by mountains, forests and waterfalls, no local people want to live. The presence of National Road 55 has turned the quiet area of the past into a pleasant and loving place for people.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said that National Road 55, which was officially inaugurated and National Road 10, which is under construction, is a corridor of Cambodia's potential national economic artery.
