Today is International Women's Day, March 8: Understand the history of this day, what is it for?
In 2020, the 109th anniversary of International Women's Day, Cambodia is organizing this day under the theme "Peace, Promoting Gender Equality."
The concept of International Women's Day first appeared at the turn of the century, in which the world of industrialization was a period of expansion and turmoil, rapid population growth and fundamentalist ideology.
A brief history of this event is arranged in the following chronological order:
According to a statement from the Socialist Party of America, the first National Women's Day is celebrated throughout the United States on February 28. Women continued to commemorate this anniversary until the last Sunday of that month in 1913.
The proposal was unanimously approved by a conference of more than 100 women from 17 countries, including the first three women to be elected to the Finnish parliament. No specific date has been chosen for the celebration of this day.
In addition to the right to vote and the right to serve the state, these women demanded the right to work, the right to receive training, and the right to end discrimination in employment. Nearly a week later, on March 25, a tragic fire at the New York City Triangle Blast Triangle Factory claimed the lives of more than 140 factory workers, most of them immigrants. Italian and Jewish.
The event had a profound impact on labor law in the United States, and the disaster-stricken working conditions were sworn in during a series of ceremonies to mark International Women's Day.
As part of the pre-World War I peace movement, Russian women celebrated their first International Women's Day on the last Sunday in February 1913. Elsewhere in Europe, on or about March 8 of the following year, women gathered to protest against the war or to show solidarity with their sisters.
With 2 million Russian soldiers, Russian women once again chose the last Sunday in February to strike for "food and peace." Political leaders have opposed the timing of the strike, but the women are still moving forward.
The growth of the international movement, reinforced by the four United Nations Global Women's Conferences, has helped mark the anniversary as a rallying point for mobilizing forces for women's rights and women's participation. In the political and economic process.
International Women's Day is increasingly becoming a time to reflect on the progress made, to call for change and to celebrate the acts of courage and commitment of ordinary women who have played an extraordinary role in history. Of women's rights.
