(Phnom Penh): On March 6, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, presided over the celebration of the 109th International Women's Day on March 8 at the Koh Pich Center.
The International Women's Day on March 8 is celebrated every year around the world to commemorate the struggle and heroic example of women in demanding freedom and freedom from oppression. In Cambodia, the rights of both men and women were lost during the Democratic Kampuchea regime of the Pol Pot genocide.
The following are the key remarks made by Samdech Techo Hun Sen on the occasion of the International Women's Day on March 8:
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen is pleased to participate in a meeting to celebrate the 109th anniversary of the International Day of March 8, 2020 under the theme "Peace, Promoting Gender Equality" which the Royal Government will choose to show the value of peace. Hardly with the motto "Thank you for peace".
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen appreciates and highly appreciates the Ministry of Women's Affairs, the National Council of Cambodia for Women, relevant ministries and institutions, the private sector, civil society and national and international development partners who have contributed to the improvement of living conditions, rights, status, role, welfare of women and children. In particular, the provision of timely services helps victims of violence effectively, equitably and environmentally.
* Samdech Techo said that the theme "Peace, Gender Promotion" is really meaningful and important in accordance with the principles of the Royal Government, which has been focusing on the promotion and empowerment of Women and children, 50% of the total population, so that they have the potential to contribute and the economy.
* Samdech Techo stated that the International Day of March 8 today is a demonstration of the full potential of women as mothers of the world with the motto "Help women is to help themselves and" Women's rights are human rights.
* Cambodia is recognized as a country where women are actively involved in the health sector, and the rate of women participation is up to 79%, business owners 65%, although most businesses in the micro and small scale.
* In the field of education in recent years, the number of female students at the upper secondary level has increased both quantitatively and qualitatively. The result of students who got A in the high school exam was around 50%.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that the Royal Government increases the budget to the National Council of Cambodia by 500 million Riels per year to help women and girls victims in seeking justice services through the establishment of a group of Samdech Techo Sen lawyers in All 25 capitals and provinces to help find justice services for the poor, women and girls who are victims of violence in a timely and effective manner.
1. All leaders of ministries and institutions must continue to increase the number of women in leadership positions at all levels.
2. Together to prevent and intervene in all aspects of violence related to gender issues, especially the use of the Internet related to sex trafficking. Expand the establishment of multi-sectoral working groups to respond to gender-based violence in all capitals and provinces, and to collaborate on the use of data on interventions and children in order to increase the effectiveness of interventions by individual institutions.
3. The Ministry of Education and Sports must instruct all educational institutions to strengthen the implementation of discipline and to communicate with parents and guardians of students on a regular basis. All parents and guardians should pay attention to educating their children, boys and girls, study hard, be moral, and have a culture of non-violence in the family and in society.
4: Continue to invest in the education of girls at all levels, especially in science, technology, engineering, creative arts and mathematics (STEAM) to keep up with the current progress in science, technology and innovation.
5: The Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, the Ministry of Information and all media, both public and private, must continue to strengthen and promote social morality, the values of Cambodian women and families, as well as increase good educational programs that reflect the values of Cambodian women, traditions and culture more widely. More will have to consider the impact and dignity and girls.
6: Together to prevent the spread of new HIV and ask for non-discrimination, especially for women beer promoters, including LGBT people.
