More than 6,000 hectares of people's farmland in Pursat province will have enough irrigation water after the project to rehabilitate the irrigation system and release water in 3 locations has been completed
(Pursat): Mr. Prum Kantheal, Deputy Director of the Technical Service Center for Irrigation and Meteorology of the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, said that more than 6,000 hectares of people's farmland in Pursat province will have sufficient irrigation water after the rehabilitation project. Three parallel irrigation and drainage systems, namely, Damnak Ampil sub-project, Wat Luong sub-project and Wat Chre sub-project, have been completed.
He added that the three sub-projects have been rehabilitated and improved by nearly 80 percent, which is scheduled to be completed by June 2020.
Mr. Prum Kantheal said this on the evening of March 4, 2020 at the JICA-led journalists' visit to inspect irrigation and drainage restoration projects west of the Tonle Sap Lake. In Pursat province.
Mr. Prum Kandel added that the benefits of the dam project were that before the construction project, farmers had difficulty using water to irrigate their farmland, and after the construction of the three sites in Pursat province. Ready to irrigate their land a lot.
He said that all irrigation and rehabilitation projects are located in three provinces: Battambang, Pursat and Kampong Chhnang, with six sub-projects, including: Reamkon in Battambang, Po Canal. Located in Battambang, Damnak Ampil, Wat Luong, Wat Chre in Pursat and Lumhach in Kampong Chhnang.
