(Phnom Penh): Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum, President of the Senate, this morning, March 5, 2020, received a delegation of women leaders at the national and sub-national levels, led by Dr. Ing Kantha Phavi, Minister of Women's Affairs. To pay a courtesy call on the celebration of the 109th anniversary of the International Women's Day on March 8, 2020. The ceremony was held at the Senate.
Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum stated that the Senate, which is the legislature, has the function of reviewing and commenting on the text of laws passed by the National Assembly to protect the rights and interests of all citizens. These laws are understood and respected by the people through widespread dissemination by the legislature and the executive as well.
In particular, the Senate has a mechanism to review and monitor the text of the law in order to ensure gender responsiveness to serve equitable benefits to the people of Cambodia, both men and women, through the Committee on Health, Social Affairs, Veterans, Youth Rehabilitation, Vocational Training and Women's Affairs. Of the Senate led by Lok Chumteav Mean Sam An today.
Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey added that the Senate would like to support the strategy of promoting gender equality and empowering women and girls, as well as the Royal Government, which is firmly committed to implementing this work and hopes that in the future, Cambodia will achieve development goals. Sustainable 2030, especially the fifth goal, which states "achieving gender equality and empowering all women and girls."
Mrs. Ung Kantha Phavi reported to the ceremony on the work results of the Ministry of Women's Affairs. She stressed that progress related to gender equality has had positive results in many areas, leading to a gradual change in the mindset and views of society.
She said that the Royal Government has created opportunities for all people, especially women and children, to have equal opportunities and benefits as men and boys, to promote family society, to provide equitable investment in education for sons and daughters to have limited education. Highly have technical skills and occupations to develop their family economy as well as achieve health advancement.
The Minister emphasized that gender inequality in Cambodia has developed in all areas, including politics, economy and society. Public and Political Sector The number of women in decision-making positions has steadily increased in both quantity and quality in positions and ranks from one mandate to another.
According to the Minister of Women's Affairs, the number of women in public service has increased from 32 percent in 2007 to 41 percent in 2019. The number of women holding positions from municipal, district, Khan level to director general and equal level is 25%, women are governors: 1 woman is deputy governor, deputy governor is 26, women is mayor, district is Khan There are 194 female deputy governors of districts and Khans.
In 2019, four female diplomats will serve as ambassadors and 55 female advisers, secretaries and accountants at the country's embassies and consulates.
She said that in terms of the economy, the World Economic Forum 2020 report shows that Cambodia ranks 25th in 2020, compared to 45th in 2018 out of 153 countries. Women's participation in the economy is active.
The rate of women participating in the high-level workforce will reach 79 percent in 2017. Women Entrepreneurship Increases 65% of registered small and medium enterprises, with women as managers or owners.
