Armed Forces and Forestry Administration crack down on illegal timber storage and confiscate about 20 cubic meters of timber in Tbeng Meanchey district, Preah Vihear province
(Preah Vihear): Brigadier General Kang Sao Kun, Commander of the Preah Vihear Provincial Gendarmerie, in cooperation with Tbeng Meanchey Forestry Administration Sangkat officers, raided an illegal timber warehouse at Boss Thom village, Promeru commune, Tbeng Meanchey district, Preah Vihear province. At the same time, the force confiscated about 20 cubic meters of type 2 timber.
In fact, on the afternoon of March 3, 2020, Brigadier General, Commander of the Preah Vihear Gendarmerie, led the Tbeng Meanchey District Gendarmerie in cooperation with the Forestry Administration to crack down on illegal timber stockpiles while traders were preparing to lift vehicles. At the above location point.
Currently, the confiscated timber has been transported to be stored at the Tbeng Meanchey Forestry Administration to measure the exact amount.
Svay Rieng CamControl Finds Boracic Chemicals in Banh Duc Dessert and Some Defective Goods at Chipho Market, Bavet City
(Svay Rieng): In order to ensure food safety for the people, CamControl Svay Rieng branch officials in the morning of March 4, 2020, in cooperation with the Provincial Department of Commerce and local authorities, inspected goods, food products and packaged goods. In Chi Phu Market, Bavet City, Svay Rieng Province, 1 kg of Boracic Chemical was found in Banh Duk dessert and 8 kg.
Mr. Pheng Nil, Head of Svay Rieng CamControl Branch, said that the inspection activities of the professional officers inspected the food at the stalls in some stalls and used the first test kits on 26 samples of food. On food products to find banned chemicals that harm people's health.
He said that as a result of the inspection, the officers found 1 kg of banned chemicals in Borax dessert, 8 kg of Borax and 8 kg of defective pastry and 24 cans of French fries out of date. After use, the professional officer made a record of confiscating the goods and storing them for later incineration.
Yet the problem of clean water for Phnom Penh residents does not seem to be answered yet. Water shortages for Phnom Penh residents, especially on the outskirts of Phnom Penh and in the center of the capital with high-rise homes, may not have been the case during the dry season this year. But the crisis does not seem to have an effective response yet.
In the suburbs of Phnom Penh, those who want to use water must set the alarm clock between 3 and 4 o'clock in the morning to get up to filter the water in the bucket. If it is too late, there will be no water.
Water outages are not as difficult as power outages. People in Phnom Penh now live a modern, civilized life, all appliances from cooking water to cooking are all using electricity. Therefore, if there is a power outage, there will be great suffering.
According to the Water Supply Authority, the problem with the water supply is not that Cambodia has a shortage of water like when there was a shortage of electricity last year, because Cambodia has up to 500 kilometers of the Mekong River, which is an unknown source of water supply. Dry. But the problem is that the capacity of the water treatment plant in Phnom Penh is still not responsive to the sharp increase in the number of consumers as well as housing.
However, people who face water shortages in Phnom Penh strongly hope that the Royal Government will take special measures such as solving the problem of lack of electricity to solve the water shortage crisis in Phnom Penh.
In particular, it is expected that the Royal Government will release a budget to establish more water treatment plants to increase the efficiency of water supply in Phnom Penh.
March 04, 2020 People on the outskirts of Phnom Penh, Ministry of Mines encourage everyone to donate blood to save lives
(Phnom Penh): To celebrate the International Women's Day on March 8, the 109th anniversary of the Ministry of Mines and Energy, on March 4, 2020, in collaboration with the National Blood Transfusion Center, organized a voluntary blood donation program for the purpose of supporting And rescue victims or anemic people across the country.
This humanitarian and charitable program is conducted with the participation of all leaders and civil servants at all levels of the Ministry of Mines and Electric Power of Cambodia and the Electricity Authority of Cambodia. This is the fifth time that the leaders and civil servants of the Ministry of Mines and Energy have joined together for social activities for humanitarian work.
Mr. Dith Tina, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Mines and Energy, encouraged all leaders and civil servants at all levels of the Ministry of Mines and Energy, as well as the people to donate blood. He says donating blood is very important because a block of blood can save a person's life.
"So sometimes we give once, we hope we save someone's life," the senior official said. "Therefore, in general, the Ministry of Mines and Energy will continue to provide more often to save more lives."
Mr. Dith Tina said that usually in three to four months, they can donate blood once, so they can donate blood about three to four times a year. He added that if you all think that one year can save the lives of three or four people, we will try to continue together because people give more blood and give more regularly to save lives. Better the same.
Please note that the Ministry of Mines and Energy on March 4, 2020 has been organizing a blood donation program on the 4th floor of the Ministry. This event is a contribution to the promotion of health and social welfare, as well as a confirmation of the generosity and compassion of the leaders and staff of the Ministry of Mines and the Electricity Authority of Cambodia to all compatriots who need blood. Emergency in case of emergency.
