World Wildlife Day is celebrated to showcase the rich animal and plant life on the planet and to promote the values of these biodiversity that have benefited the people.
The day also reminds everyone of the need to take action against wildlife crime and the depletion of certain species by human activities that could affect the economy, the environment and society.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that the Royal Government of Cambodia pays great attention to the conservation and conservation of biodiversity of animals and plants in protected areas to preserve them for generations to come. Another face.
He said that in rescuing wildlife and conserving wildlife, rangers who guarded protected areas removed 17,175 traps from the forest in 2019, an increase from 2018 until 5,000 traps, of which the province that released the most traps was Koh Kong province with 7,074 traps in 2019.
"The wildlife and plants on this planet are invaluable in terms of ecosystems, society, economics, science, education, culture, linked to human life and sustainable development," said Neth Pheaktra. Well. ”
He added that some of the world's endangered wildlife and birds are present in Cambodia and on behalf of the Ministry of Environment would like to urge people to participate in the protection and conservation of wildlife in Cambodia and called on people to stop hunting. Capture illegal wildlife that faces the law.
The Secretary of State also called on people not to eat wild animals because they are infected and do not have proper control, which can affect their health. He said that after the 19 outbreak of Kovid, China called on people not to eat wild animals and not to trade wild animals.
According to experts, eating wild animals is not good for health, but can lead to infections as well as infections. He said that park rangers and relevant authorities in the provinces regularly inspect the markets and restaurants are not allowed to sell wild meat.
He added, "I would like to appeal to the people, if you see wildlife meat sold, please contact professional officials to take legal action."
"Cambodia, as a member of the United Nations, is ready to cooperate internationally with all countries in conserving natural resources and preventing and combating illegal wildlife trafficking," he said.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added, "Cambodia has been actively involved in the fight against illegal wildlife trafficking, and Cambodia has also cooperated as an international partner in preventing and suppressing illegal wildlife trade. The world.
