Eighth, all units, including the Department, must pay attention to implement the recommendations of Samdech Techo Hun Sen on strengthening the effectiveness of preventing the import of animals, agricultural products and illegal agricultural materials. Samdech Techo Hun Sen: No Amendment to the Constitution to Transfer Positions to Successors (Video inside) No.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen said this after a group of people on the telegram who wanted a constitutional amendment to pave the way for the transfer of the Prime Minister from Samdech to his son when there is a problem. One.
At the graduation ceremony for Asia-Europe University students on March 2, 2020, the Prime Minister considered this initiative to be wrong. Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that in the Cambodian Constitution, even the succession to the throne is not transferred to the heir.
According to the Cambodian Constitution, the successor to the throne shall continue to be elected by the Royal Council. The composition of the Royal Council consists of: President of the Senate, President of the National Assembly, Prime Minister, Samdech Preah Sangkhareach, Mahanikaya and Dhammayutnikaya, First Vice President and Second Vice President of the Senate, First Vice President and Second Vice President of the National Assembly .
Samdech Techo Hun Sen asked those who suggested the amendment of the Constitution to transfer the position of Prime Minister to this heir to re-study the Constitution. Samdech Techo stressed that Cambodia is an electoral country, there is no transfer of power.
It should be noted that Article 125 of the Cambodian Constitution states that the post of Prime Minister, when completely vacant, will be re-appointed to a Council of Ministers. In particular, if the Prime Minister is vacant from time to time, the acting Prime Minister shall be appointed temporarily.
During the graduation ceremony this morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also reaffirmed that there would be no amendment of the Constitution for the electoral system in the National Assembly from the 50 plus 1 system to other systems.
In 2006, the Cambodian Constitution was amended to change the electoral system in the National Assembly from two-thirds to 50 plus one. The move aims to avoid a political stalemate, which was initially stipulated that the formation of a new government would require a two-thirds majority and, if not, a coalition government.
The amendment changed from a two-thirds system to a 50-plus-one system at the time, with the support of Sam Rainsy, the former president of the Sam Rainsy Party, and his party members.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that the election system in the National Assembly 50 plus 1 is a system to help prevent the stalemate in the formation of the Royal Government and to protect the losers who want to lead the Royal Government.
