On that occasion, the provincial governor also requested the people to cooperate with local authorities to implement the policy of safe villages, communes, eliminate cases of drug offenses, gambling and respect for traffic laws, etc. for peace and prosperity. The authorities decided to build a steel bridge to the Phnom Tnaot, Phnom Pok wildlife sanctuary after the wooden bridge was burnt down.
(Preah Vihear): Authorities have decided to build a steel ballet bridge to Phnom Tnaot Phnom Pok Wildlife Sanctuary after a wooden bridge to the wildlife sanctuary was set on fire due to wildfires and arson. This bridge is located in Trapeang Thlok village, Sdao commune, Sangkum Thmei district, Preah Vihear province.
The budget for the construction of the steel bridge was supported by Senior Minister Meas Sophea, Chairman of the Royal Government Working Group for Preah Vihear Province, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment and Mr. Prak Sovann, Provincial Governor. Church. This was confirmed by Mr. Samleang Try, Director of the Department of Public Works and Transport.
Mr. Samleang Try also pointed out that the team of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Preah Vihear Province has now started the construction of this steel bridge. The bridge is expected to be completed in about seven days. Mr. Samleang Try also said that the bridge is about 48 meters long and more than 4 meters wide. Pillars and bridges are made of iron and wood.
Please note that before reaching the construction of the ballet bridge, the place was built by a wooden bridge, but was subsequently burned. Some say the fire was caused by a wildfire, while others claim that wildlife criminals were the arsonists.
Please note that Phnom Tnaot-Phnom Pok Wildlife Sanctuary is located in Sangkum Thmei District, Preah Vihear Province and Siem Reap Province with a total area of 42,097 hectares. Phnom Tnaot Phnom Pok Wildlife Sanctuary is claimed to be rich in wildlife and natural resources.
In the last two years, four wooden bridges have been built by the Ministry of Environment to cross the Phnom Tnaot Phnom Pok sanctuary. Each bridge is about 50 meters long or longer.
