After that, the Minister inspected the construction of the CPP headquarters in Ponhea Leu district, which has been under construction since January 7, 2020, to date, has achieved about 30% and the construction of more than 10 km of paved roads. Phnom Bat, Chrey Las and Chhwang communes, the first phase has achieved about 70%. Legal Awareness: People with limited ability to act
(Phnom Penh): In general, everyone is fully aware of their rights so that they can exercise this right to freely carry out activities without compromising the rights of others and contrary to the law.
However, some of the actions taken were legally recognized and fully validated, and some of the actions taken by others were not legally recognized as valid or required the consent of the beneficiary. Others to validate those activities, for example.
The fact that some of the actions taken and are not recognized or invalid is due to the fact that those who have taken those actions are considered to be incompetent or have limited ability to act. Therefore, we should understand what kind of person is defined by law as a person with limited ability to act?
Therefore, the legal awareness program of FRESH NEWS in collaboration with the Cambodian Bar Association for International Cooperation would like to raise the topic of "People with limited ability to act" to present to the following students:
According to the Civil Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia, a person who is restricted in his ability to act refers to any physical person who lacks the ability to understand and judge his legal activities due to his age, memory and intelligence. .
There are 3 (three) types of people with limited ability: minors, people under general guardianship and people under the guardianship. The term "action" also refers to the expression of will, unilaterally or through a contract.
Minor refers to a person under the age of 18 (eighteen) years. However, not all minors are limited in their abilities, just as minors who have been granted immunity are not limited in their abilities and are considered to have reached the age of majority.
The person with parental authority (father or mother) has the rights and duties to nurture the body of a minor child. However, in the case of a minor without a father or mother, a guardian regime must be established for the minor to appoint a person to govern the minor with the same rights and duties as the parent.
In principle, any action taken by a minor without the consent of the parent will be canceled by the parent of the minor. But some activities can not be canceled, such as accepting gifts, signing contracts free from obligations, buying and selling daily necessities, managing property with parental permission, doing business with the permission of Parents.
2 * For people under general guardianship:
The General Guardian is in charge of supervising the person under the General Guardian and the court appoints the Guardian arbitrarily and shall do so at the request of the Supervisor, General Guardian, Sub-Guardian, Spouse, Relatives in 4 levels. , Commune Chief, Sangkat Chief, Prosecutor or other interested person.
In principle, the activities of people under the general guardianship can be canceled only if those activities are related to daily life, such as buying food, food, daily necessities and so on.
Nutritionist: A person who has the role of supervising and caring for people under the nutritionist. The court shall select the sponsor arbitrarily, while the procedure for the nomination of the sponsor shall follow the same procedure as the nomination of the general guardian.
All in all, some of the disabled persons, such as minors, general guardians and sponsors of the activities of some of these individuals, were prohibited by law and canceled by the authorities. But there are also some activities that the law allows, such as daily activities and some activities that are required by law to be authorized by the person who governs the regime.
