(Preah Vihear): Mr. Kor Som Bunsoeut, Acting Governor of Preah Vihear Province and Mr. Khan Khon, President of the Association of New News Mission, together with Mr. Sok Santara, Governor of Chhep District, as well as local authorities, handed over water wells to 3 villages in Chhep district and 2 villages in Tbeng Meanchey district to respond to the Royal Government's policy on water supply.
On the afternoon of February 28, 2020, on that occasion, the Acting Provincial Governor Ko Som Bunsoeut brought a message from Mr. Long Sovann, Chairman of the Provincial Council and Mr. Prak Sovann, Governor of Preah Vihear Province, who always took care of Always consider the well-being and daily life of the people in Preah Vihear province.
Mr. Ko Som Bunsoeut said, "Cambodia, under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen as Prime Minister, has achieved peace and development throughout the country in order to improve the living standards of the people and the development of the country." "There is participation from all stakeholders, including community participation from many national and international associations."
In fact, the wells received by the people as a gift from the Gospel Mission Association, with Mr. Khan Khon as President and Advisor to the Ministry of Cults and Religious Affairs, provided 16 wells with a total budget of $ 28,800. Mr. Prak Sovann, Governor of Preah Vihear Province.
Mr. Ko Som Bunsoeut also requested the people who benefit from the use of this well water to help protect and protect the common interest and long-term use to help meet the needs of the people. Lack of drinking water, especially in response to the Royal Government's policy on clean water supply. Mr. Neth Pheaktra: Phnom Penh uses more than 10 million plastic bags per day
(Siem Reap): Worryingly, the whole world consumes more than 400 million tons of plastic per year, and Phnom Penh alone consumes more than 10 million plastic bags per day. This is according to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment on the night of February 28, 2020.
Asian countries throw more plastic into the sea, with India increasing its annual plastic waste by 5.6 million tons. The Indian government seems to be taking a stand on the issue. A law banning plastic bags has been in place since 2009. But practicality is not there. Indians continue to use plastic bags and other packaging materials made of substances.
According to a United Nations report, since 1950, plastic products have surpassed all other products. Most of these products are disposable products such as bags, bottles, buckets, plastic lids, sandwich boxes, packaging, straws or plates, spoons, etc. The latest figures show that about 500,000 million plastic bags are used each year, which means 10 million bags per minute.
Of all plastic products, only about 9% are recycled. 12% were burned. The remaining 80% is dumped into nature, such as streams, before flowing into the sea. It takes thousands of years for plastic waste to decompose.
Understanding the consequences and participation of individuals in society is essential. It is not easy for people in society to give up their old habits without understanding the consequences. But this is on the enforcement of the law and the publicity of the state authorities, which must be effective.
