Phnom Penh Senior Officials Urge Relevant Officials to Raise Awareness on the Serious Impact of Sweet Drinking on People
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Keo Sok Pisey, Deputy Governor of Phnom Penh, urged the relevant officials to work together to raise awareness of the serious impact on health due to excessive consumption of sweet drinks to people across the country, especially citizens. In the community.
She made this statement at a seminar on reducing the consumption of sweet drinks this morning, February 28, 2020.
Keo Sok Pisey said: “In Phnom Penh, drinking sweet drinks is a problem. In 2013, the World Health Organization (WHO) School Survey found that 52% of urban youth consumed at least one can of sweetened beverages a day, and more than 60% saw advertisements for sweetened or ready-to-eat drinks. When watching TV or playing video games.
All these factors are why the relevant officials must work together to make efforts to disseminate through this training and to educate the people directly at the local level. But there must also be the participation of our people together to reduce the habit of drinking this part to less and better.
Ms. Keo Sok Pisey said that drinking too much sugary drinks can increase the risk of cardiovascular disease, heart disease, heart attack, diabetes, cancer (cancer), tooth decay and disease. Obesity, when overweight, can lead to other diseases.
Phnom Penh Capital Hall is committed to continuing to raise awareness of the effects of excessive consumption of sugary drinks and to reduce the consumption of sugary drinks in schools by strengthening the implementation of the guideline No. 18 IECN dated 2 May 2019. Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports.
Therefore, today's workshop is to implement the guidelines of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, with the relevant authorities of 40 schools (principals, teachers, parents, students and school food sellers) and health officials. To help reduce the sale and consumption of sweet drinks in schools.
