In connection with the outbreak, the World Health Organization does not recommend any trade or travel restrictions.
While the current outbreak of coronavirus continues, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the World Tourism Organization (WHO) are committed to working together to guide tourism and travel responses, the statement said. Will the Covid-19 crisis.
The World Health Organization (WHO) is working closely with global experts, governments and partners to quickly gain a scientific understanding of the new virus, monitor its transmission and its effects, and provide guidance to countries and communities. Global community on measures to protect health and prevent the spread of the virus.
The joint statement said that the tourism sector has a strong commitment to put the people and the well-being of the people as a priority, and international cooperation plays an important role in ensuring that the sector can effectively participate in the prevention of new coronary heart disease.
The World Tourism Organization (WHO) and the World Health Organization (WHO) are working closely with other partners to help states ensure effective implementation of health measures and minimize unnecessary barriers. To tourism and international trade.
The response from the tourism sector requires an appropriate balance of public health threats and based on local risk assessments by encouraging the participation of all sectors of the tourism value chain, including public institutions, private companies and the private sector. Tourists comply with the recommendations and general guidelines of the World Health Organization.
The World Tourism Organization and the World Health Organization are ready to work closely with all communities and countries currently affected by health emergencies to contribute to building a better and stronger future. Restrictions on travel that deviate from the above spirit can cause unnecessary disruption to international traffic, including negative impacts on tourism.
At this challenging stage, the World Health Organization joins the international community in maintaining solidarity with the affected countries.
In a press conference organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government on the morning of February 27, 2020 on the progress and further direction of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, Mr. Heng Sour stated that among the 200 factories facing this may Unemployed about 160,000 workers.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Labor said: "The problem with the corona effect is that we know that in March there will be nearly 200 factories that could face a shortage of raw materials. Affecting about 160,000 workers.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Labor added that in Cambodia as well as in countries that produce this garment also face the shortage of raw materials, but through the efforts of the Royal Government of Cambodia hope that Cambodia will have. Prioritize to get this raw material first.
According to Heng Sour, as of this morning, February 27, 2020, there are 10 factories and have requested the suspension of some parts of the production line, and the number of workers suspended in this section is about 3,000. While the Ministry is coordinating to make sure that the factory settles the salaries and other benefits for the suspended workers in accordance with the recommendations of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
