Please note that this is the first program that mentions pest control after cooperation in mosquito control in 2019.
Senior official of the Ministry of Interior: Good governance reform is the main task that the Royal Government is focusing on
(Kampot): Mr. Ngy Chanthol, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior, said that good governance reform is one of the main tasks that the Royal Government is paying close attention to to make public administration more efficient and meet the needs. Of the people in line with the national economic growth, which is constantly evolving at a rapid pace.
Secretary of State Ngy Chanthol continued that in this context, public administration, especially sub-national administration, must strengthen its capacity to have more specific functions and appropriate resources to carry out the functions transferred responsibly and High efficiency.
The reform of the provision of public services to the people is part of the decentralization and deconcentration reforms, especially the transfer of functions and resources from ministries and institutions at the national level to sub-national administrations that are close to the people. Promote service delivery and local development to meet the needs of the people with quality, efficiency, transparency, accountability and social equity.
The Secretary of State also said that the establishment of a one-stop-shop mechanism in the sub-national administration is a form of decentralization reform and promote good governance in the provision of services through the collection of services as needed. It is necessary for the people to come together in one place and hand over to the administrative level that is close to the people to be responsible for providing services to the people.
He said that this mechanism has been implemented by many countries for a long time to make it easier for applicants to meet the needs of local people by reducing the complexity and complexity of procedures and procedures. Administration to facilitate citizens in requesting services from public administration.
At present, in Cambodia, we have established OWSOs and Citizens' Offices at the municipal, district and khan levels and have delegated 211 administrative services to provide services in 10 specialized fields by the end of 2019.
There are 115 OWSOs in 203 of the 253 municipalities, including 27 municipalities, 74 district administrations, and 14 district administrations, accounting for 57% of the total. Number of municipalities, districts and Khans throughout the country.
On that occasion, Mr. Governor Chiao Tai also said that: Kampot Provincial Administrative Office is currently providing administrative services to people in 14 sectors through technical coordination of specialized units. In the province from September 2018 to December 2019, the OWSO provided services to a total of 10,619 people and received a total income of more than 1,573 million riels.
Ministry of Economy Introduces Information Technology System for Public Financial Management
(Phnom Penh): The information technology system for public financial management has been officially launched by the Ministry of Economy and Finance to increase the efficiency of managing the implementation of revenue and expenditure of the national budget. The information technology system for public financial management, which was announced to be launched on February 24, 2020, is submitted to the 17 ministries and institutions and the budget units, which are the 13 full transfer bodies.
"This is another step towards contributing to the goal of preparing for the digital economy and responding to the fourth generation of the Industrial Revolution set out in the Royal Government's Rectangular Strategy - Phase IV," said Hean Sahip.
He highlighted the important role of the FMIS system in strengthening efficiency, accountability, transparency and supporting the management and monitoring of budget and financial performance, while all ministries and institutions are required to implement program budgets and The establishment of a budget unit, in particular, supports the progress towards the fourth phase of the Public Financial Management Reform Program, "Achievement Accountability" in 2021.
The lecturer encouraged and highlighted some important points to the participants from the ministries and institutions in continuing the cooperation to implement the FMIS project, including:
1: The key departments of the Ministry of Economy and Finance must continue to actively participate in helping to guide and support new users in the FMIS system, to disseminate and provide comprehensive guidance on the link between budget implementation and Utilization of the system as well as budget implementation procedures and strengthening the accountability of financial transactions in the FMIS system.
2. Ministries and institutions that use the FMIS system, must continue to increase their professional capacity, both in public finance and in using the FMIS system with a high sense of responsibility and ownership in managing their budget and finances. Term of use of FMIS.
4: All FMIS users must participate in strengthening the accountability of the use of FMIS by carefully reviewing the data recorded in the FMIS system and using the information from the FMIS system as a basis for managing and implementing the budget through analysis. , Monitor, inspect and prepare reports on budget loans and budget implementation.
