The European Commission's decision will take effect in August 2020 if the European Parliament does not reject it. Samdech Techo Hun Sen has announced that the Royal Government has already put in place a number of measures to address the challenges facing garment and footwear enterprises as well as handbags in Cambodia.
China is a country where up to 70% of investors are investing in textile factories in Cambodia. China is also an importer of products made in Cambodia.
On February 24, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen delivered a special message to his compatriots on measures to address the challenges facing the Cambodian economy caused by "COVID19" and the suspension of the preferential treatment on February 24, 2020. He recalled that during a meeting with Chinese President Xi Jinping on February 5, Samdech asked the Chinese government for three points to help resolve the suspension of the preferential treatment.
The three proposals made by the Prime Minister to the Chinese President are: 1: Continue to encourage Chinese investors who are doing business in Cambodia to continue to operate their factories, 2: Continue to encourage more investors to come to Cambodia and 3: Open a bigger market for Cambodian goods.
Currently, Cambodian exports to international markets are up to 14 billion US dollars, of which Europe is about 4 billion US dollars. More than $ 10 billion went to other countries, including China, Japan and South Korea. Samdech Techo Hun Sen has said that the suspension of Europe's 20 percent preferential system will not kill Cambodia.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that "Cambodia can not be killed in any way. Cambodia has been silenced in the past, Cambodia is trying to survive to this day. "Thus, I hope that the Chinese friends will continue their efforts to help us, although not all, but can alleviate the difficulties and accelerate the Cambodian economy."
The European Union (EU) has decided to suspend Cambodia's preferential treatment under the pretext of undermining respect for human rights, political rights and democracy, and to demand that Cambodia improve. This morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen reaffirmed that he did not protest for European concessions, let alone 20 percent, even if the 100 percent tax system was suspended, he accepted.
