This morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen delivered a special message to the Cambodian people regarding the latest situation
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on Monday, February 24, 2020, will deliver a special message to all Cambodians regarding the latest situation.
The message was delivered at the Peace Palace and was broadcast live on Cambodian media.
After delivering a special message and opening a Q&A forum with the media, the Prime Minister will then chair the third semi-annual meeting of the Supreme Advisory Council.
The Supreme Advisory Council was established by Royal Decree dated September 6, 2018 at the request of Samdech Techo Hun Sen after the 6th National Election. The Supreme Advisory Council is composed of 16 of the 20 political parties that participated in the election.
The Supreme Advisory Council is a mechanism for consulting and advising political parties to the Royal Government outside the parliamentary framework in order to improve work efficiency for the benefit of the people and the potential of social development.
After its establishment, the Supreme Advisory Council acted in accordance with its duties and responsibilities, including reviewing and advising on draft laws before submitting them to the Royal Government for review and decision.
The Supreme Advisory Council also summoned the leaders of various ministries and institutions to inquire about some irregularities under the jurisdiction of those ministries and institutions. In addition, members of the Supreme Advisory Council have been active in resolving issues for the people, including land disputes in the provinces.
The Supreme Advisory Council said that in the 16 months since its inception, members of the Supreme Advisory Council have visited more than 400 missions across 25 capitals and provinces and made reports accompanied by evidence on various issues. Which it collected and submitted to Samdech Techo Hun Sen in nearly 200 cases. In nearly 200 reports, the Supreme Advisory Council received 32 responses from Samdech Techo.
In the past 16 months, the Supreme Advisory Council has held 12 meetings to review various draft laws and organized 13 meetings to present and discuss input with each ministry delegation. .
In 2019, the Royal Government provided more than $ 1 million to the Supreme Advisory Council for the cost of its activities. But the Supreme Advisory Council has spent nearly $ 870,000, leaving more than $ 450,000.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the initiator of the establishment of the Supreme Advisory Council, expressed great satisfaction with the mechanism of the Supreme Advisory Council, which has contributed greatly to solving social problems, including the inactivity of some officials. .
