The National Blood Transfusion Center needs nearly 200 units of blood a day to save lives, while the demand for blood is currently insufficient.
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Ly Sovit, Deputy Director of the National Blood Transfusion Center, stated that at present, the National Blood Transfusion Center needs an average of 200 units of blood per day, while the need for blood to save lives has not yet met the actual needs.
This statement by Dr. Ly Sovit was made on the occasion that the Ministry of National Defense and the National Blood Transfusion Center organized a blood donation program under the theme "Give Blood, Give Love" on February 20, 2020 in the presence of Ms. Dam Darini, Secretary of State of the Ministry of National Defense, with the participation of more than 300 Cambodian officers and officers from friendly countries at the National Defense University.
Dr. Ly Sovit said: "Currently, the demand for blood is not enough to save the victims who need blood. At present, we only get 20 or 22 percent of the volunteers who donate blood, so at 78 percent. "All of them donate blood instead, while on average they need 200 units of blood a day to save lives."
Dr. Ly Sovit expressed hope that if ministries, institutions and units organize frequent blood donation programs, they will be able to cope with any victims who need blood to save their lives.
However, Dr. Ly Sovit said that through his observations, more and more young people and volunteers have come to donate blood, especially through the activities of Mr. Hun Many, President of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia. (CPPCC) and Mr. Kim Rithy, President of the CPPCC in Kandal Province, as well as other leaders who regularly lead the youth in inspiring the blood donation movement to the National Blood Transfusion Center to help people. Victims and so on.
In addition, Dr. Ly Sovit confirmed that currently the National Blood Transfusion Center uses blood to help victims who need blood can only resist but can accept. Donating blood is very beneficial for human beings, especially to be aware of their health problems. He also called on all Cambodians to donate blood to save lives.
Ms. Dam Darini said that the volunteering to donate blood shows the participation of the soldiers of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces in addition to the core work, which is a strong force in national defense and patriotism involved in social work. Especially in donating blood today, it comes from the true volunteerism of the people.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of National Defense confirmed that your blood donation today is not only to help patients who are in need of blood, but also to get medical check-ups. Be aware of blood pressure, cholesterol levels, blood tests, which are both important factors in maintaining health and to ensure that blood can be used for donations.
