A group of 200 young Cambodian researchers understand and research genocide in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): In 2020, a group of about 200 young researchers from various colleges across Cambodia will study and research genocide in Cambodia under the Documentation Center Village Project of the Documentation Center of Cambodia.
1: Collect and preserve the memories of Khmer Rouge survivors 2: Increase reconciliation and dialogue between survivors and the younger generation born after 1979 regarding Khmer Rouge history 3: Increase public awareness About the personal experiences of survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime.
Vocational training for these 200 young researchers will take place in a two-day workshop focused on research and writing oral history stories. The purpose of this workshop was to provide basic skills on how to conduct oral interviews with victims and survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime, as well as techniques for extracting data from interviews and writing arrangements.
The two-day workshop will be conducted according to a research project in which a group of young researchers will conduct practical interviews with survivors in their villages, with expert staff from the Documentation Center of Cambodia and international consultants. Lead. Each young researcher will be interviewing four survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime in their commune.
Mr. Pheng Pong Rasy, Director of the Village History Project of the Documentation Center of Cambodia and a lecturer in the two-day workshop, said that the Documentation Center of Cambodia aims to enable young Cambodians to gather information and work as a team to organize the series. Research questions, produce and compile your own historical story.
Mr. Pong Rasy added that these young researchers will be divided into writing and video recording groups, which will be required to study a variety of topics, focusing on "daily life in The Khmer Rouge era. DC-Cam provides students with research ideas, sources, references, and local research costs.
Young researchers, who own the writing and the short video, can use their achievements on a personal basis at any time, such as submitting to a competition through various programs.
Keo Kim On, a second-year history student at the Royal University of Phnom Penh from Kandal province, described the Documentation Center of Cambodia's Village Historical Project, which will make me aware of the history of events that took place during the Khmer Rouge regime. I will be interviewing people who went through the Khmer Rouge regime, which is an important source of study materials. This project will help me to create research questions, write research projects.
Chanrath, a second-year history student at the Royal University of Phnom Penh from Pursat province, described: “This village history project will help increase my ability to research, write historical stories and get to know historical sites locally. "The topics I will be writing are documents for students, researchers and the general public."
In this Village Historical Project, the Documentation Center of Cambodia has five other locations in the provinces besides offices in Phnom Penh for young researchers to explore, including: 1: Anlong Veng Peace Center 2: Research Office and Khmer Document Library Red Prey Veng 3: Khmer Rouge Research and Library Office, Takeo Province 4: Veal Veng Reconciliation Center 5: Khmer Rouge Khmer Rouge Research and Library Office, Kampong Cham Province.
