(Kampong Chhnang): A 2,700-meter-long canal from Stung Ta Kuon to Boeung Kamplanh is located in Lvea and Talat villages, Svay Rumpear commune, Kampong Leng district, Kampong province.
Meanwhile, the contractor is using machinery to dig the canal deeper and expand it according to the district's plan to irrigate more than 2,000 hectares of farmland, while the back of the canal has been cleared and compacted. Trail for transporting rice from the field to the base as well.
The rehabilitation of the canal, which the Royal Government has helped, makes the people who own the dry rice fields happy, and everyone hopes that in the coming years, their paddy fields will no longer lack water like in previous years.
Mr. Soeng Son, Chairman of the Water Management Committee in Talat Village, on the evening of February 18, 2020, said that in this area, every year, dry rice farming always lacks water because the old canal is dry and shallow, which people do not have. Unable to recover, and every year, some hectares of rice fields were damaged, withered and many hectares of sugarcane were damaged.
But in 2020, at the request of the people, the head of the Royal Government also reduced the budget to help restore this canal, which is 2,700 meters deep and bigger to bring water from Stung Ta Kuon to irrigate more than 2,000 hectares of people's farmland. The back of the canal has also become an easy way to transport rice to their base.
Meanwhile, the manager of You Sarith Company, which is the contractor for the restoration of the canal, said that the bottom of the canal is 2 meters wide, 8 meters wide and the length is 2,700 meters, and to this day, the company restores the canal. More than 40% and the project will be completed by mid-March.
Mr. Sok Rin, Director of the Agriculture Office of Kampong Leng District, also said that the canal rehabilitation plan is funded by the commune fund plus the budget of the head of the Royal Government through the Aspay program (program to promote new agricultural techniques that are resistant to climate change).
Mr. Sokrin stated that the canal rehabilitation project is very important for farmers in Svay Rumpear commune, who used to have a shortage of water every year, but now they are no longer worried because Hopefully, when the canal is rehabilitated, their rice fields will have a high yield, which will be able to harvest the family's supply.
