The Cambodian Footwear Association stated that it was "disappointed" by the decision of the European Commission to propose the withdrawal of part of the EBA on about 30 percent of all imports. "Exporting Cambodian shoes to the European Union is a huge setback for the sector, which has provided employment to Cambodians, mostly women."
The Cambodian Footwear Association says the footwear sector has played an important role in Cambodia's economic development by creating jobs, raising wages and helping lift hundreds of thousands of people out of poverty.
The Cambodian Footwear Association emphasizes, "At the moment we regret the EU decision and the unforeseen negative impact on the footwear sector, loss of employment and disproportionate impact on the workforce. We. "We urge the European Union to reconsider this withdrawal and to refrain from further proceedings."
Please be informed that on 12 February 2020, the European Commission (EU) issued a decision to cut parts of the EBA. According to the private sector, the deduction of parts of the preferential system is worth more than 100 million US dollars on 41 Cambodian products. The decision will take effect in six months, in August 2020, if approved by the European Parliament.
