(Phnom Penh): Ms. Por Sokun, owner of Vattanac Handicrafts, revealed the main goal of establishing local handicrafts to supply the local market, reduce imports and create jobs for young people.
She said that the decision to create her own handicraft was made after she was angry with herself for not getting any knowledge about the import, so she decided to learn how to produce and open her own handicraft.
Speaking at the weekly special guest program, Ms. Por Sokun said, "It has been five years since I imported perfumes, but recently I have decided to open a handicraft business by Myself. This local handicraft was created to contribute to the local economy and help Cambodian children have jobs and know their own skills.
She continued, "In opening this Vattanac handicraft, I bought recipes from abroad, imported raw materials and produced body soaps, face scrubs and soaps, as well as shampoos, and all soaps. High quality, not inferior to imported products. "This contributes to boosting the local economy, having its own brand to supply the local market."
Ms. Por Sokun said that this handicraft has received support from the people of Cambodia by buying and using and reselling after seeing that the quality of local products are better than imported products.
Ms. Por Sokun stated, "The decision to open Vattanac handicrafts to make perfumes so that Cambodians know that in Cambodia also has its own factory and brand without having to buy a brand from another country and to enhance the image of the country and the product. The quality is not bad, because the formula is composed with a clear study.
She said that she had some worries before deciding to open this business, but those worries were overcome and not afraid, with faith in the Cambodian people, in which about 20-30% of people support local products. . However, we still face challenges because Cambodians are less interested in Cambodian products.
"If people support local products, our country will grow, help people create jobs, reduce migration and boost Cambodia's economy and image," she said.
