Mr. Chhour Chandoeun stated that there are 216 pagodas in Kampong Chhnang province, 14 pagodas, 1475 monks, 631 abbots, 66 primary schools, 1109 ordinary students, 1 secondary school, 262 ordinary students and secondary Buddhists. There is one high school with 53 ordinary students, 58 churches, 105,281 Muslims and 377 Catholic churches with 3,710 members.
Separately, Wat Otumpor has a total area of 27,541 square meters, east of the road, south of the house and west of the rubber road, about 12 kilometers long, and this pagoda was built in 1922 by Chief Inspector Om.
It should be noted that according to the report of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration, Wat Otumpor during the Pol Pot era was completely destroyed like other pagodas in the Kingdom of Cambodia. After 1979, only one Buddha statue and an ancient stupa remain. Recently, the construction of the pagoda has started to be rebuilt and the achievements in this pagoda have continued to this day.
(Central): Dr. Chhim Sarath, Director of the Asian Health Care Fund (AHF) in Asia, said that the celebration of International Condom Day will help remind the general public that HIV / AIDS is still a crisis. Serious for the whole world and this day is the best way to raise awareness and fight the spread of HIV.
Dr. Chhim Sarath said this on the evening of February 15, 2020 in the "International Condom Day" under the theme "Condoms are always up to date" held on the banks of the river in Takhmao.
Dr. Chhim Sarath added that AHF is celebrating International Condom Day with the aim of: continuing to raise awareness and promoting the importance of condoms in the prevention of sexually transmitted diseases, HIV and AIDS. Unplanned pregnancies show the world that condoms are essential to prevent HIV and STIs and that access to condoms must be easy and adequate worldwide.
He added that the AIDS Health Care Foundation (AHF) is proud to have partnered with the Royal Government of Cambodia in the fight against HIV / AIDS and is committed to continuing to work with the Royal Government of Cambodia and its partners to achieve "participation". Let's join together as one nation in preventing the spread of HIV "in achieving the National Strategy to End HIV / AIDS 2025.
Mr. Chhim Khin Dareth also stated that regular and proper use of condoms can not only effectively prevent STIs and HIV, but also prevent unintended pregnancies. In the world today, more than 1 million people are infected with STIs every day, and every year about 80 million people become unintended.
He added that condoms are used up to 98% effectively to prevent HIV and other sexually transmitted diseases. It is estimated that over the past two decades, Cambodia has prevented the spread of HIV to about 900,000 people and protected about 600,000 people from dying from HIV.
At the same time, Minister Mam Bunheng also called on the people to implement the eight essential self-defense measures to prevent the new coronary heart disease as follows:
1. Avoid close contact with people with acute respiratory illness or suspected patients.
2. Wash your hands often (with soap and water) or alcohol, especially after direct contact with the patient or the environment.
3. Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing with a towel or paper towel.
4: Stay more than 1 meter away from people with acute respiratory symptoms or those who cough or sneeze.
5: Avoid close contact or go to crowded places in case of unnecessary or markets or farms with live animals or wildlife or places where the virus is known to be circulating.
6: Eat well-cooked food, especially meat and eggs.
7. Health workers who provide care to patients must adhere to measures to prevent infection in health facilities thoroughly and properly to avoid transmitting this disease to individuals.
