(Phnom Penh): After several months of construction, on the morning of February 15, 2020, Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) launched the 180-kilometer Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville high-voltage transmission line. In addition to commissioning the transmission line, Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) has also commissioned the 230-kilovolt Chamkar Luong substation at the Sre Ambel junction.
This power line can transmit power at a voltage of 230 kV. According to Electricite du Cambodge (EDC), the transmission line is very important in transmitting power from Sihanoukville coal plants to Phnom Penh and beyond.
The new transmission line contributes to the transmission of power as the current transmission line passing through Kampot and Takeo provinces is overloaded, unable to fully transmit the volume produced by the coal plant in Sihanoukville. In particular, a new 150-megawatt coal-fired power plant will be operational in early 2020.
Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) has confirmed that the new transmission line will be able to transmit an additional 1,000 megawatts of power, which will be used to build a series of coal-fired power plants in Sihanoukville.
Separately, Chamkar Luong 230 KV substation is one of the six substations that was first launched under the concessional loan from the Government of the French Republic through the French Agency for Development, which was jointly supervised by Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) and consultants. From Electricity France (EDF).
The substation will play an important role in distributing and receiving power to the south and west between the coal plants in Sihanoukville, Phnom Penh and Koh Kong, which are rich in hydropower.
Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) also highlighted that the new transmission line and substation is another new achievement in the electricity sector that will help boost the national economic development by increasing the availability of sufficient, stable and sustainable energy supply.
