Cambodian private sector regrets EU decision to withdraw parts of preferential tariffs offered to Cambodia under EBA
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian private sector, represented by 20 major institutions, including European business people in Cambodia, has issued a statement expressing regret over the European Commission's decision to exclude parts of The preferential system provided to Cambodia under the "Everything But Arms (EBA)" agreement.
"The Cambodian Chamber of Commerce and the signatories representing the private sector in Cambodia have expressed regret over the Commission's decision," according to a Cambodian private sector press release obtained by Fresh News on 15 February. "Europe for the removal of parts of the preferential treatment system provided to Cambodia under the Universal Arms Exclusion Agreement (EBA) after it inspected on 12 February 2019."
By imposing a 20% tariff on Cambodia's exports to Europe worth about $ 1.3 billion, Cambodia will have to pay about $ 140 million a year in taxes to Europe this year. This is mentioned in the press release.
"In the future, Cambodia will continue to benefit from trade concessions of 80% of its exports to the EU," the private sector said in a statement. We therefore call on the European Commission and the Royal Government of Cambodia to continue to engage in dialogue on issues raised in the European Commission's oversight. "
