Three more unions in Cambodia issue statement expressing regret over European Commission decision on EBA
(Phnom Penh): Three more unions in Cambodia on February 14, 2020 issued a joint statement expressing regret over the European Commission's decision to suspend parts of the EBA to Cambodia. .
The National Union of Cambodian Workers (N.L.C), a professional organization representing workers in the textile, garment and footwear sectors working in factories, enterprises and establishments in Cambodia, expressed deep disappointment and regret over the European Commission's decision yesterday. February 12, 2020 on the temporary withdrawal of parts of the EBA "All Goods" except Cambodia.
The National Union of Cambodian Workers (N.L.C) considers that this measure is inconsistent with the realities of the remarkable progress of the human rights and labor rights situation in Cambodia. In the past, as a professional organization representing workers, we have always worked closely with the Royal Government and agencies representing international labor organizations and development partners on projects and activities related to the promotion of labor rights.
The Union of Trade Unions for Labor Rights (Y.S.L.R) also expressed deep disappointment and regret over the European Commission decision. The union said the EU decision was in line with EU standards, which were politically motivated.
Decisions that are biased and do not fully consider the real situation in Cambodia have affected the interests and livelihoods of Cambodian workers. In this sense, as a union operating in Cambodia, it is unacceptable for the European Union to act unjustly against Cambodia and its Cambodian workers.
The Federation of Trade Unions (U.F.W.S) also issued a statement expressing its disappointment and regret over the decision. The union considers this an injustice for the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Cambodian workers despite this bias.
The Federation of Workers' Safety Unions also called on all Cambodian workers to continue to respect and enforce the law, as well as to continue to support programs and measures put forward by the Royal Government of Cambodia to prevent and address potential challenges. Eventually, in particular, training programs and skills development to strengthen the capacity or acquire new skills that lead to new job opportunities instead of the current job that may face change.
