Senior Minister Kun Kim: Under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, all disasters were resolved in time for the people
(Phnom Penh): General Kun Kim, Senior Minister in charge of Special Missions and First Vice-Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, said that in 2019, Cambodia is facing weather risks caused by drought and lack of water for people's livelihood. People in 14 provinces affected 342,641 hectares of paddy fields and damaged 67,663 hectares. Natural disasters in 2019 killed 95 people and 95 cattle.
This statement was made by Senior Minister Kun Kim on February 14, 2020, on the occasion that the Senior Minister chaired the meeting to review the work of disaster management in 2019 and the direction for 2020 of the National Committee for Disaster Management. Disaster with the participation of many relevant ministries and institutions.
Senior Minister Kun Kim said that the floods were caused by the August rains in Kampot, Sihanoukville, Mondulkiri and Ratanakkiri provinces, and in September the local storm affected the northwestern lowlands and highlands. Floods, rains. After the flooding of the Mekong River from mid-August to the first week of September, the affected provinces are Stung Treng, Kratie, Tbong Khmum, Kampong Cham, Prey Veng, Kandal.
The senior minister added that the drought and floods have affected and damaged the social sector, agricultural production and infrastructure, and for the damage figures, the National Committee for Disaster Management has received from the Disaster Management Committee in all capitals and provinces, so about It may differ from the figures of the Ministry of Expertise, which requested a discussion on the report table as provided and find a solution.
Senior Minister Kun Kim confirmed that natural and man-made disasters in Cambodia in 2019, and in early 2020, there will be building collapses in Kep province and there will be a global epidemic of coronavirus pneumonia. Makes the world anxious. Therefore, the growth of disasters is not able to keep us quiet until a disaster occurs and then we solve it.
