French grape farmers in Sre Ta Chey village, Teuk Phos district, more than 4,000 clumps have not been successful due to the lack of water sources
(Kampong Chhnang): Farmers planting French vines in Sre Ta Chey village, Aphivath commune, Teuk Phos district, are worried as the dry weather has left three of their pumping wells without enough water to irrigate more than 4,000 bunches of vines. He, who is more than a year old, was interrupted for about three months and did not get any results.
But what he is disappointed with is that this dry season, the weather was so dry that the French grapes that were harvesting were missed for about three months because three wells in his field had no water source. Enough for irrigation until the rains come and there is enough water.
Mr. Mat Phally stated that in the rainy season, there is enough water per day, he can cut his grapes from 5 to 7 kg, which retail at the local price of 15,000 riel per kilogram and wholesale to Moey in Phnom Penh. 1 kilo costs 10,000 riel, but in the modern market it costs $ 6 per kilogram.
Farmer Matt Phally also instructed local farmers that if they want to plant this French vine, find a place with sufficient water source in both rainy and dry months by taking care of it and watering it every day. Succeeded.
This vine is known as an emerald vegetable without chemicals that have good nutrients and especially foreigners, they like to eat it. As for the resale market in supermarkets, no matter how much they buy, it is not difficult to sell, while retail sales in the local market are also high, from 12,000 riel to 15,000 riel per kilogram.
