(Phnom Penh): Public health researcher Tong Soprach has been conducting research for more than 10 years, from 2007 to 2020, on the perceptions of young people on February 14. According to the researcher, during the past 10 years, up to 12% of young Cambodians considered February 14 as the day to show love, with the male lover giving gifts or flowers, and the female lover giving their bodies in return. Instead.
Misunderstandings over the meaning of February 14 led young Cambodians at the time to call it "Valentine's Day" and caused a great deal of remorse for young people, especially women. Due to accidental pregnancy, HIV infection deprives the student of the opportunity to study and causes depression.
But after the active participation of the media, relevant institutions such as the Ministry of Education, the Ministry of Culture, schools, parents, etc., introduced the meaning and methods of self-defense to children as well as young people to better understand the day. February 14, also known as Valentine's Day.
According to research, young people today have changed from showing love through giving up their bodies to showing love to their parents, teachers, siblings, friends and so on. In addition, young Cambodians are also turning their attention to social work, such as donating blood.
All these factors made Mr. Tong Soprach, a well-known social analyst, hope that the situation of young Cambodians on February 14 will be able to return to normal. But he continues to call on all relevant institutions to continue to educate young people to better understand the meaning of this Valentine's Day. To prevent the danger of misunderstanding the meaning of this day, as during 10 years ago.
It should also be noted that this problem does not occur only in Cambodia, but Cambodia's neighbors, especially Thailand, have experienced this problem more seriously than Cambodia, and to date, neighboring countries have also declined. Awkward on the occasion of this Valentine's Day as well.
