Minister Veng Sakhon Chairs a Meeting to Review the Progress of Contract Agricultural Production
(Phnom Penh): This morning, February 13, 2020, the Agricultural Production Contract Coordinating Committee held a meeting to review and discuss the "Progress Report, Challenges and Further Directions for the Implementation of Contract Agricultural Production" for the second half of 2019. Presided over by Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and Chairman of the Agricultural Production Contract Coordination Committee.
The meeting was attended by representatives of relevant ministries and institutions, leaders of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, representatives of the Capital-Provincial Administration, members of the Agricultural Production Contract Coordinating Committee, Mr. Oknha, Ladies and Gentlemen, National and International Guests, Private Sector, Development Partners And agricultural communities, a total of about 500 people.
Mr. Veng Sakhon expressed his gratitude and appreciation for the presence and efforts of the members on behalf of the family members of the Agricultural Production Contract Coordinating Committee, Department of Agriculture and Industry, which is the Secretariat of this committee. Grow Asia, which co-sponsored and facilitated the meeting, also appreciated the significant interest of the Grow Asia representatives.
He said that, as in previous years, the committee organized this meeting in order to discuss jointly between the members of the committee with inter-sub-national administrative institutions, as well as relevant national and international organizations, development partners, the private sector and all agricultural communities. Monitor, evaluate and understand the progress, challenges encountered in the past and the continuation of joint efforts
In addition, to find constructive solutions by focusing on the vision of accelerating the implementation of the Royal Government's policies within the framework of effective reforms to increase productivity, diversification and commercialization of agriculture to promote a competitive national economy. Achieve successful poverty reduction and move towards the goal by 2030 in the context of the Fourth Industrial Revolution and digitalization.
In this new context, contract farming is really playing an important role in linking the four main actors: producers, including private processors, development partners and the public sector. So far, the coordination of agricultural production contracts has been 104 contracts for rice production, 4 contracts for corn, 1 soybean, 154 broilers for raising chickens, 143 pigs for meat, 23 vegetables, 18 cassava and 2 palm sugar contracts.
In addition to the achievements of the Agricultural Production Contract Coordinating Committee, the Committee also faced a number of key challenges during the implementation of the contract, and the meeting addressed a number of issues raised by the private sector, development partners and the community.
Other issues include: building infrastructure, supporting production lines, electricity prices, income taxes, transportation costs, water supply, low-interest financing, certification of origin and registration of crops. - etc. The committee will prepare a detailed report and submit it to the Royal Government for information and high decision.
Please be reminded that Sub-Decree No. 36 Dated 24 February 2011 on Contract Agricultural Production of the Royal Government of Cambodia was established for the purpose of:
