The number of migrants crossing the US-Mexico border dropped to 74.5% due to Donald Trump
MEXICO CITY (Reuters) - The number of migrants crossing the US-Mexico border in the past nine months has dropped to 74, Mexican Foreign Minister Marcelo Ebrard said on Wednesday. 5% after a bilateral agreement to curb the flow of migrants between the two countries. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua in the morning of Thursday, February 13, 2020.
In early January 2020, the number of migrants crossing the border between the two countries was only 36,679, which is the lowest figure compared to the number of migrants in May 2019, which was up to 144,116 were recorded by Mexican authorities.
In May 2019, as the number of migrants crossing the border reached an all-time high, US President Donald Trump called on the Mexican government to take action. Has tightened to ensure and strengthen security along its borders, especially with Central American countries.
"The goal of these measures is to reduce the high risk that migrants may face," he said. He added that to ensure security for the people, the Mexican government has led a multinational program to promote investment in Central America and set up a special committee to help migrants find temporary employment.
