(Prey Veng): Residents of Prey Khlot village, Smaong Tbong commune, Kamchay Mear district, Prey Veng province, expressed their gratitude to Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, for providing a 7-room concrete school building to a primary school. Prey Ky, as the number of students increases every year.
Mr. Steng Sokhom, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the National Working Group for Kamchay Mear District, representing Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, presided over the groundbreaking ceremony for the construction of this school building on the morning of February 11, 2020. Prey Ky Primary School is located in Prey Khlot Village, Smaong Tbong Commune, Kamchay Mear District.
Prey Ky Primary School was established in 1979 in the location of Wat Prey Ky, with 2 houses equal to 4 rooms, built of galvanized iron, with the budget of the pagoda's chief priest and the Buddhists at the foot of the pagoda. The school was continuously developed until 2001, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng and she provided a school building with 5 classrooms, an office and a 380-meter-long fence.
Currently, Prey Ky Primary School has 7 teachers and 450 students. Due to the increasing number of students every year, seeing the announcement of additional school buildings, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng assigned Mr. Steng Sokhom, Chairman of the National Working Group to assist Kamchay Mear District to study and decided to provide a school building. There are 7 rooms built of stone.
At the groundbreaking ceremony, Mr. Steng Sokhom said that Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng always paid attention to the well-being of the people, he always built roads, schools, hospitals to the local area, which is the need of the people in order Improve livelihoods, well-being and human resource development, especially by pushing schools closer to people's homes.
Mr. Steng Sokhom stressed why the school should be moved closer to people's homes, because this school is a nursery for people's knowledge, and when the people's children have knowledge, people can work together. Occupations and occupations are more developed and better than the uneducated.
The President of the National Working Group continued to emphasize that the reason for training human resources is to give people general knowledge and skills, because whatever we do, if we have the skills, the business will always be successful and Get high yields. Therefore, all citizens must send their children to study and finish college, because Kamchay Mear district has a university in this district.
Mr. Steung Sokhom continued that the establishment of schools and universities in Kamchay Mear district is a long-term wise vision of the CPP leaders who have made efforts to spend their own capital as well as the support of philanthropists. Kamchay Mear exists throughout the university.
Currently, Kamchay Mear district has 51 public educational institutions, including 39 primary schools, 92 school buildings with 372 classrooms, 8 secondary schools, 21 school buildings with 91 classrooms, and 2 high schools with 8 school buildings. Propose 80 classrooms and a university with 13 school buildings divided into 5 colleges, 1 community development institute and 17 dormitory buildings.
Mr. Steng Sokhom said that in response to the need to increase the number of students every year in 2019, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng provided 7 people in Kamchay Mear district, equal to 39 rooms and 7 offices. Renovation of 8 school buildings, 35 rooms, fences, gates and construction of 2 commune administrative buildings.
The principal of Prey Ky Primary School thanked Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng for always caring for the people of Prey Khlot village, even though the village is remote. In response to this merit, the principal expressed his commitment to teach students to educate and educate students to avoid mischief and strive to maintain this noble achievement.
Responding to the requests and requests of the people, the Chairman of the National Task Force for Kamchay Mear District accepted the request to build roads and canals by asking the commune council to study this project.
He also explained that the Royal Government of Cambodia has reduced the budget for communes to around 120,000 dollars per year, but that there is a budget for development of about 70,000 dollars per commune for 2020, so each commune can raise The project to build roads and canals in the commune villages is a priority, so the commune can review this proposal and act as a stage.
Regarding the electricity issue, the President confirmed that he had already submitted it to Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) and asked the district authorities to check which communes and villages did not yet have a state electricity network to connect electricity to the people. Get electricity in the villages.
In the afternoon of the same day, Mr. Steng Sokhom also attended the inauguration of various achievements in Wat Rattanak Seila called Wat Krabao Thmey in Krabao Commune, Kamchay Mear District, which includes the library of Buddha Khoeun Moha Po Pruek, drainage system, garden and other achievements. Give the field of Buddhism.
Mr. Steng Sokhom expressed his congratulations for the many achievements that have taken place in Wat Rattanak Seila, also known as Wat Krabao Thmey, with the generous donations from philanthropists and Buddhists near and far to achieve the achievement of many achievements. It is in the common interest of monks and Buddhists to adhere to Buddhist beliefs.
The Chairman of the National Working Group continued that the policy of the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the current Prime Minister of Cambodia, makes the people happy, which is different from more than 40 years ago, where people live in fear. Suffering has no rights at all.
He added that the well-being of the people in all areas, including religion, can only happen if the country is peaceful and politically stable. Thank you for the peace and join in thanking all the peace by working together to maintain lasting peace so that Cambodia can move towards the progress of development in all areas, the people live together in harmony forever. To the next generation.
(Phnom Penh): The Head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, on February 12, 2020, issued a circular setting out five measures to improve the effectiveness of the implementation of the human rights reporting mechanism in Cambodia for the United Nations. .
1. Since human rights work is a cross-sectoral work, all ministries, administrative institutions, capitals, provinces and professional units at all levels must cooperate closely and responsibly with the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, including research, education and dissemination of human rights. Receiving and resolving complaints and investigating and monitoring the human rights situation in prisons and other places of detention and detention.
2. All Ministries, Administrative Institutions, Capital-Provinces and professional units at all levels must cooperate fully and responsibly with the Cambodian Human Rights Committee in the process of preparing the Human Rights Report in Cambodia for the United Nations. For example, providing information and data, attending meetings, and providing regular response reports.
4. Relevant ministries and institutions shall co-organize and send representatives to join the Cambodian Human Rights Committee to defend the human rights report under each treaty at the United Nations at the request of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee.
5. At the request of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, the Office of the Council of Ministers requests a budget from the Royal Government's Special Budget to send a delegation of the Kingdom of Cambodia to defend the Cambodian Human Rights Report at the United Nations for the Cambodian Human Rights Committee. And delegates representing necessary ministries and institutions.
Regarding these five measures, Mr. Chin Malin, Spokesman of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, thanked the Head of the Royal Government for always giving high value and attention to human rights work in Cambodia. He considered that this is a new turning point in the work and the human rights situation in Cambodia, which is the result of the work and efforts of all leaders and officials of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee.
Ministry of Interior: Allegations by associations and civil society organizations that Cambodia lacks the will to amend the law on associations and demand the repeal do not reflect the truth
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Interior on February 12, 2020 issued a statement rejecting the statement of some associations and civil society organizations on February 10, 2020 that the Royal Government of Cambodia is unwilling to Amended the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations and demanded the repeal of this law. The Ministry of Interior considers that the allegations made by such associations and civil society organizations do not reflect the truth.
The Ministry of Interior said that the non-profit legal entities limited in the Civil Code are only general principles. Thus, the Civil Code does not prohibit the enactment of a law on associations and non-governmental organizations.
The Ministry of Interior added that during the implementation, listening to the opinions of stakeholders, the Ministry of Interior agreed to accept the proposal to amend some articles. Four meetings to discuss amendments to the proposal have already been held, starting during November 2019, until it was agreed to amend nine articles out of 17 articles proposed by associations and organizations. Not the government.
"The Ministry of Interior hopes to continue discussions on the remaining articles in the near future," he said. Therefore, the statement that the Royal Government is not willing to amend this law does not reflect the facts as stated above. According to a statement from the Ministry of Interior
