Australia continues to support demining in Cambodia, so far Australia has provided $ 100 million to help clear landmines
On behalf of Samdech Techo, President of the Mine Action Authority, Senior Minister Ly Thuch expressed his deepest appreciation and gratitude to the Government and people of Australia for their support and participation in the Royal Government of Cambodia in the recent humanitarian demining work in Cambodia. In particular, contribute to reducing the poverty of the Cambodian people.
Mr. Pablo Kang stated that Australia has supported demining work in Cambodia since 1994 and has spent more than $ 100 million to help clear landmine-threatened areas and provide land for housing and Safe agriculture for people living in remote areas and people living in areas heavily affected by landmines.
Swiss government provides $ 6 million more in demining support
(Phnom Penh): The Swiss government has decided to provide an additional $ 6 million to support demining by 2025, according to the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Swiss assistance was presented by the new Swiss Ambassador to Cambodia, Carin SALERNO, during a meeting with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, on the morning of February 12, 2020. .
Koy Kuong, a spokeswoman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, said the ambassador highlighted past cooperation in which the Swiss government has focused on health, a sector related to children's health. Ms. Carin SALERNO stated that Switzerland continues to support Kantha Bopha Hospital, even though it is now run by Cambodians.
The spokesman also reminded that the Swiss government has supported Kantha Bopha Hospital since 1994. By 2018, the Swiss government has provided about 60 million Swiss francs (about 60 million US dollars) to Kantha Bopha Hospital.
On that occasion, Mr. Prak Sokhon thanked the Swiss government for its contribution to economic and social development, especially in the field of health care for Kantha Bopha Hospital. He also thanked the Swiss government for its assistance in demining work, which the Swiss government continues to assist.
The Head of Diplomacy stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia has always made sure that Kantha Bopha Hospital is always operational and expands its scope. In that, our Royal Government contributes 10 million USD per year, and in addition, Cambodia also has the Kantha Bopha Fund, which donates about 5 million USD per year, while the Cambodian Red Cross also donates 1 million USD per year. Also.
According to Koy Kuong, Switzerland is interested in agriculture, especially food security, where Switzerland has provided some financial assistance, especially technical assistance to key provinces that produce good quality rice. A lot. She also helps with hospitality training, and in the past she has worked with the Ministry of Interior to support projects called decentralization.
On that occasion, the two sides also discussed future trade cooperation, noting that the current trade volume between Switzerland and Cambodia is fluctuating, meaning that some years are high and some years are low. Especially if you look at 2018, see a high of about 100 million dollars, while in 2017 only 20 million dollars, and 2019 see a slight decline.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs stated that "this is the point at which the two sides will find a way to make steady growth." Therefore, the Ambassador to increase the amount of investment promised that in 2020, she will lead the heads of major Swiss companies to Cambodia to examine the investment potential.
Related to another issue is the tourism sector, which both sides also looked at ways to attract more Swiss tourists to Cambodia, which is also a factor driving the national economic growth. In that, she urged that there will be training for Cambodian travel agents as well.
In response, the Ambassador expressed her pleasure and stated that she was honored to be appointed Ambassador to work in Cambodia. She said her task was to make the relationship that has existed since 1963 even stronger and deeper.
In the past, she also monitored the development process in our country, and she also stated that although the Swiss Embassy in Bangkok, Thailand, but now Switzerland has a cooperation office in Phnom Penh to work directly with the Royal Government of Cambodia. . In addition, Switzerland also has a consular affairs agency in our country to facilitate the work of Swiss people or tourists.
Ministry of Economy Prepares Draft Action Plan for Implementing Public Procurement System Reform Strategy 2019-2025
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of February 12, 2020, the Ministry of Economy and Finance organized a workshop on the preparation of a draft action plan for the implementation of the Public Procurement System Reform Strategy 2019-2025 chaired by Mr. Hiv Sovann Anou. Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and Mr. Chhim Sareth, Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Public Procurement.
Welcoming Mr. Hiv Sovann, he expressed his appreciation for the cooperation of the General Department of Public Procurement of the Ministry of Economy and Finance in organizing this workshop in order to consult and collect more input to ensure the preparation of the draft. The action plan for the implementation of the public procurement system reform strategy 2019-2025 is comprehensive and can be implemented smoothly.
He emphasized that the strategy for reforming the public procurement system 2019-2025 was approved by the Royal Government of Cambodia on May 08, 2019 and announced to be implemented together with two other strategies, including:
Taking this opportunity, Dr. Chhim Sareth further recommended that in order to achieve the strategic objectives and objectives of the public procurement system reform 2019-2025, it is necessary to prepare a detailed action plan for the implementation of the system reform strategy. Public Procurement 2019-2025.
This draft action plan document is organized into five sections, including:
* First: Preparation and approval of procurement plan * Second: Public procurement implementation * Third: Monitoring and evaluation of procurement implementation * Fourth: Strengthening the independence of the grievance redressal mechanism and Fifth: Supporting infrastructure These include: improving the legal framework related to public procurement, preparing public procurement manuals, studying the use of electronic public procurement, and capacity building.
