Japanese companies to bid for garbage collection in Phnom Penh
Mr. Mikami Masahiro confirmed this during a courtesy call and discussion with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen at the Peace Palace this morning, February 12, 2020.
In this meeting, Samdech Techo Prime Minister said that garbage collection in Phnom Penh is in transition because the old company could not get this job because Phnom Penh expanded from about 300 square kilometers to 600 square kilometers. Samdech Techo added that in the near future, the Phnom Penh Capital Administration and the Ministry of Economy and Finance will put up for auction, so Japanese companies should bid to help collect garbage.
In response, the ambassador stressed that Japan is paying attention to the problem of garbage after helping with clean water. He said that the garbage problem is necessary and Japan has three companies participating in the bid to collect garbage.
A report by the Ministry of Environment shows that the total amount of waste generated in Cambodia is more than 10,000 tons per day and nearly 4 million tons per year. Of that, 65% is organic waste, 20% is plastic bag waste and the rest is solid waste and other waste. For Phnom Penh, about 3,000 tons of garbage is generated per day.
The confirmation of Mr. Mikami Masahiro was made during a courtesy call and discussion with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen at the Peace Palace this morning, February 12, 2020.
During the meeting, Mr. Mikami Masahiro also stated that the Japanese government is also considering providing funds to help Cambodia buy cars and equipment for the ASEAN Summit in 2022.
The Japanese ambassador also confirmed that Japan is currently cooperating with the Ministry of Mines and Energy in the field of electricity to promote cooperation in the field of electricity. Japan is also helping to prepare a master plan for Japanese investment in Cambodia, as well as discussing and studying the master plan for investment expansion of Sihanoukville port, as well as Japan's contribution to the drafting of construction laws and the preparation of legal standards. Next.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen thanked the Government and people of Japan for their assistance to Cambodia in continuing to assist Cambodia in demining and unexploded ordnance, as well as arranging for Cambodia to make the Asia-Europe and ASEAN Summit a success. . Samdech Techo also thanked Japan for its assistance in drafting the law for construction.
